select the right ad  for the right person at the right price  impression is when the ad is actually  displayed then user click and video  completes our useful metrics for  advertisers 10 billion events a day  that's more than 100 thousand events per  second that we have to collect store and  process within our systems and another  important metric is 10 we are only a  team of 10 to give meanings to to all  these events that's why we are more than  happy to delegate to bigquery the  complexity that comes with that scale  now let me tell you where we come from  what made us choose big Korea teats and  how it slowly became our main data  biasing system so all these events are  first stored into Kafka before bigquery  our data by plan was following the  principles of the lambda architecture  and the batch layer we were storing the  events on it's free and then spark  strobes were aggregating these events  into Cassandra tables the speed layer  was doing some of these aggregations in  real time using storm this model was  complex we had to maintain two code  bases and it did not scare well the cost  of Cassandra was getting out of control  and we were limited in the features that  we could build upon it in 2016 which was  bigquery to overcome these limitations  we also chose to simplify the data  pipeline with a single data flow process  to load all the events into bigquery we  also chose an ELT approach as opposed to  an ETL approach meaning we first stole  all the events into bigquery and then  use bigquery only to process these  events let's have a look at this LG so  data flow is loading all these events  into bigquery using batch mode and  writes one table per batch every about  30 minutes the events you see in these  tables are unordered then the elt  bigquery takes over here and we do  several jobs inside the query from these  tables I'll give you two examples the  first one is a simple job that takes all  these events and opens them into a  single federated table and this table is  very important for our data analysts to  run exploration queries or for the  beginning purposes the second job I will  show here is the rollup job this job has  two purposes first it groups events by  one table per hour so we can know where  to look at the events and the second  role of this job is to aggregate these  events so we can query them much faster  let's take an example with five events  that we have so each of these events  have a timestamp an ad ID and a website  ID and the name of the event in this  example we want to group to group into  three dimensions the how her from the  timestamp the ad ID and the website ID  and we want to compute three matrix  which had a number of impression of  start and clicks in this example we've  grouped these five events into two lines  so that's a 2.5 compression compression  ratio whereas in real life our  prediction job can perform a 70 X  compression ratio  now before big hurry to do that at scale  we had to run complex parks jobs written  in Scala with bigquery it's been  simplified into this simple sequel query  as you can see we are grouping by our  dimensions and computing the matrix in  the Select statement in production we  have 60 dimensions and 150 metrics I can  tell you the query is much longer but  it's not more complex it's just as  simple as that in production this query  runs in 1 minute and 40 seconds despite  having to group by 60 dimensions and  process 100 million rows per batch and  the good thing is it's saturated the  equivalent of our 3000 slots for only 8  seconds it means we have plenty of room  to run multiple queries like this at the  same time speaking of slots I now  explain why we chose flat rate and how  we manage it the most common pricing  model for bigquery is the undermount  pricing where you pay $5 per terabyte of  data you scan flat rate is an  alternative pricing model for 40,000 a  month you'd get 2,000 dedicated slots  regardless of the amount of data you  scan if you're scanning few enough bytes  per month it makes sense to use the  on-demand pricing low capacity planning  no commitment you only pay for what you  use  if you be rich this 40,000 a month you  have two options you can either reduce  your usage or optimize your query so to  reduce your bill or you can go fret rate  and try to make the most out of it at  its which was the second option it made  us focus on building value from big  queries unlimited power rather than  optimizing and cost control it  encouraged also other teams to use  bigquery to in three years kids  costs as as kept stable and we only had  to increase our slot capacity once from  two to three thousand and by the way a  few days ago Google announced that that  rate is available from 500 slots so the  entry price is now about ten thousand  dollars a month one of the challenges of  flat rate is workload management with  flat rate pricing you're not counting  the bytes you scan by your query you're  monitoring your slot usage at some point  you'll reach saturation of your compute  capacity that's okay that means you're  getting the most out of bigquery but you  have to make sure some of your workloads  are not impacted to merge the others you  have to make sure they are not starving  the others throwing money at the problem  is always the easiest solution so it  helps but it's not a silver bullet as  you may end up starving all that stuff  you may understand all your slots and  yes you still have no guarantee that you  some of the workloads are not starving  the others splitting different workloads  into different project helps the  bigquery scheduler give a fair share of  slots for each project this way a given  project cannot impact too much the  others in this example the orange  project is using most of the slots but  when the green and blue project needs  some they can access a fair share of it  another way to isolate workloads is to  implement your article reservation with  reservation system you can assign slots  to notes that are groups of projects and  you can make that the slaughtering  uni-directional  in this example we've isolated the most  critical projects into the critical node  and they get 1000 dedicated  shruts they can also access mm lots of  the other projects whereas the other  projects cannot have access to the  critical one this makes sure that  critical projects always have a minimum  of resource available now that I've  showcased how we use bigquery I finished  by the three major impacts that it had  at EADS it made us build projects and  feature that we were not able to do  before it's made data accessible by  everyone easily thanks to sequel which  is a game-changer when you compare it to  Jupiter notebooks and because thanks to  a UI with single sign-on and built-in  security and last but not least it made  us focus on building products and not  maintaining infrastructure we use AWS  redshift 2 and to give you an idea we  spend more time operating clusters of  hundreds of gigabytes on it  than we spend time operating bigquery  with hundreds of terabytes key takeaways  bigquery gave us performance and  simplicity it made our data accessible  flat rates drives usage with predictable  costs but requires workload management  if you want to know more about our data  pipeline our engineering blog as  phenomenal articles about it  Hollis trees are great and I'm not only  saying that because I wrote them  finally if like me you prefer sunny  sulfur friends over the 101 commutes we  are hiring  [Applause]  [Music]
 

